<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Leinster</h1><p class="st-meta">400 CE – 1171 CE (approx.) · capital Ferns</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Province-kingdom of south-east Ireland, whose exiled king Diarmait Mac Murchada invited the Anglo-Normans in and so opened eight centuries of English involvement.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Laigin, the people of Leinster, appear in the earliest Irish traditions as a major power whose kings once held the midlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-of-tara">Tara</a>. From the 5th century the rising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> drove them south, and Leinster settled into an over-kingdom covering most of the south-east, contested between dynasties such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-dunlainge">Uí Dúnlainge</a> in the north and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ui-chennselaig">Ui Chennselaig</a> in the south.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Kings ruled through tribute and alliances with subject peoples, and great monasteries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kildare">Kildare</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glendalough">Glendalough</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferns">Ferns</a> flourished. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandub-mac-echach">Brandub mac Echach</a> defeated the high king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-bolg">Dún Bolg</a> in 598. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> lay within Leinster, and from the 11th century its kings sought to control it; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-mael-na-mbo">Diarmait mac Máel na mBó</a>, who died in 1072, ruled Dublin and nearly became high king.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1166 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a> was expelled and sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Norman</a> knights led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow</a>, who married Diarmait's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aoife-macmurrough">Aoife</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford">Wexford</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. On Diarmait's death in 1171 Strongbow claimed Leinster, and Henry II's arrival brought it under the English crown.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-leinster.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Leinster" width="320" height="418"><figcaption>Image: Erigena · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Leinster-900.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 CE – 1171 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ferns</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Brandub mac Echach, Diarmait mac Máel na mBó, Diarmait Mac Murchada</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Irish, later Middle Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ireland</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Laigin, Leinster</dd></dl></aside></div>
